      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "@id": "https://www.data4thepeople.com/p/climate-globe#faq",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are the basic facts on climate change this map shows?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Four things. The world as a whole was 1.60°C warmer in August 2026 than in the Augusts of 1880-1900, and the 10-year average of Augusts is 1.22°C warmer. Land has warmed more than the oceans. The Arctic has warmed most of all, by 3°C or more in many months. And warming is not a smooth line: single months swing above and below the trend, which is why the globe lets you play through every year."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What does 1.5°C above pre-industrial mean?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It means the global average surface temperature, averaged over many years, is 1.5°C warmer than it was in 1850-1900, before fossil fuel use changed the atmosphere. The Paris Agreement set 1.5°C as the level countries would try to stay under. This globe shows the same measure for each place on Earth, month by month, against 1880-1900."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Has the world already passed 1.5°C?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Single years have. Copernicus reported 2024 as the first calendar year above 1.5°C, and August 2026 at 1.65°C against 1850-1900. The target refers to a long-term average, and by that measure the world was close to but not yet at 1.5°C as of 2026. On this globe, the 10-year average of Augusts from 2017 to 2026 is 1.22°C against 1880-1900."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why does the map use 1880-1900 instead of 1850-1900?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Because NASA's record begins in 1880. For the globe as a whole the two periods are within a few hundredths of a degree of each other. Regionally they can differ by a few tenths, which is why we label every number \"vs. 1880-1900.\""
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why are some areas gray?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "NASA reports no value there for that month. In early years that is most of the Southern Hemisphere and the polar regions. In recent months it is sea ice and a handful of cells where station reports arrived late."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is the early data measured or modeled?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Both. Where ships and stations took readings, the values come from those readings. Where they did not, NASA fills the gap with statistical estimates built from temperature patterns in better-measured decades. These are not climate model runs. The filled-in share is largest in the 1880s and 1890s, especially over the oceans, and the error bars are wider then."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Don't the corrections to old ocean readings add warming?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The main correction does the opposite. Before about 1940, most sea temperatures were taken by hauling up water in a bucket, and the water cooled as it evaporated on deck. Correcting for that raises the early readings, which makes the total warming since 1880 smaller, not larger."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What changes with the 1951-1980 baseline?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The numbers go down, because 1951-1980 was already warmer than 1880-1900. The places that warmed most stay largely the same. That view starts in 1951, so it uses none of the thinner early data. For August 2026 it shows 1.42°C of warming, compared with 1.60°C against 1880-1900."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How does this compare with satellite records?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Satellites have measured the air a few miles above the surface since 1979. From January 1979 through August 2026, the University of Alabama in Huntsville (UAH) satellite record warmed about 0.16°C per decade, and NASA's surface record about 0.21°C per decade. They measure different things, but both show warming, and both show it faster since 2004."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why does a single month look so different from the 10-year average?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Weather. One month at one place can run several degrees above or below normal, and the Arctic and Antarctic can swing by more than 6°C. Averaging ten of the same month smooths most of that out and leaves the trend."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Where does the population data come from?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The Global Human Settlement Layer from the European Commission's Joint Research Centre, 2025 estimate, at roughly one-kilometer resolution. We sum it into the 2° temperature cells and hold it fixed for every year."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often is the globe updated?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Monthly, after NASA's release, which usually lands in the middle of the following month."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is this a map of climate change or of weather?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Both, depending on the setting. A single month is mostly weather laid on top of the trend. The 10-year average is close to climate. Playing through the years shows the trend pushing the weather upward."
          }
        }
      ]
